#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133540 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/id #uuid "18e10da5-f3e1-52d2-b2c6-2a69a1bf1947" :question/math-academy-id "e-5614" :question/problem "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$7$** | **$6.5$** | **$6$** | **$5$** | **$3$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072540/assets/9da2a49a4bab645fa1b8ea3f98e6aa6135b191e121d0cc78a73b517fbc510fc0.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}7$** | **$\\color{purple}6.5$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}5$** | **$\\color{purple}3$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{red}9$ | $\\color{red}15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072540/assets/b5302251a49a45514a55d2987289548e2a55917884ff4c240be081ba512a970e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133541 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "ab93579a-f7a1-5cfe-85ce-5a4d5722e7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5615" :question/problem "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$-8$** | **$-2$** | **$6$** | **$15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$8$** | **$6$** | **$4$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $20$ | $40$ | $60$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072540/assets/3f86f03e3a32ede2ac18d6c39f62de5686a74d4050b8c1d7aa2a6fc3e2cdddbb.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}-8$** | **$\\color{purple}-2$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}15$** |\n| $x_3$ | **$\\color{green}8$** | **$\\color{green}6$** | **$\\color{green}4$** | **$\\color{green}2$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}10$ | $\\color{red}20$ | $\\color{red}40$ | $\\color{red}60$ |\n\n \n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny= \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 +\\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 15 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 40 \\\\ 60\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072540/assets/c83d300d9e6e1c1bd61fc49c4e849ab373d902beb3cd12ef501461a86bd1a08c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64e85580-f9e5-56e3-a4d5-ab4deeb9ca53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 15 & -10 \\\\ 3 & 29 & -6 \\\\ 4 & 22 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 41 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376165} {:answer/id #uuid "3e13be56-6d5a-56a8-87ec-5ee6b134bf0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376166} {:answer/id #uuid "651eb11e-b1f8-5292-a6aa-23f201a93c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376167} {:answer/id #uuid "2bef3bd3-e4e5-52ca-9294-ddb31d74151a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 29 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 41\\end{aligned}]" :db/id